# Source for the Front's heterogeneous coalition: Fakhreddin Azimi,
# Iran: The Crisis of Democracy, 1941–1953, ch. 5, pp. 250–267.
# The recurring meeting and its effects are counterfactual.
title: Coalition Meeting
new-page: true
is-card: true
tags: party_affairs
view-if: coalition_meeting_timer <= 0 and front_formed = 1
on-arrival: month_actions += 1; coalition_meeting_timer = 3

= Coalition Meeting

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front's{!</span>!} component
organizations have agreed to cooperate, but they do not
share a single membership, chain of command, or political program. A meeting
can settle one common priority for the next round of work; it cannot turn the
coalition into a disciplined party.

Choose what the secretariat will ask every component to carry back to its own
organizers.

[? if coalition_procedure != "Unsettled": The last durable arrangement was
“[+ coalition_procedure +].” This meeting can reinforce it or change course. ?]
[? if coalition_procedure_setbacks > 0: A later dispute has tested that
arrangement in public; the meeting cannot treat it as an untouched compact. ?]

- @meeting_constitution
- @meeting_dissent
- @meeting_oil
- @return_card

@meeting_constitution
title: Reaffirm the narrow constitutional program
subtitle: A narrow pledge can be defended by unlike allies, but it postpones their social disagreements
on-arrival: {!
var repeated = Q.coalition_procedure === "Narrow common mandate";
Q.coalition_procedure = "Narrow common mandate";
Q.coalition_procedure_stage += 1;
Q.iran_party_relation += repeated ? 1 : 4;
Q.independent_nationalists_relation += repeated ? 1 : 3;
Q.toilers_dissent += repeated ? 3 : 1;
Q.constitutional_legitimacy += repeated ? 1 : 3;
!}

The meeting adopts representative elections and constitutional government as
the language every component can repeat.
{!<span class="term term-constitutionalist">!}Iran Party{!</span>!} members
and {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}independent nationalists{!</span>!} leave with a clear common case, while some
{!<span class="term term-social-democratic">!}Toilers' Party{!</span>!}
organizers question what
the narrow formula postpones.

The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} sounds more
coherent in public. Its unresolved programmatic
differences remain inside the room.
[? if coalition_procedure_stage > 1: At this stage of the coalition's life,
another narrow declaration settles less than the first. Allies who wanted a
broader program are less willing to treat postponement as compromise. ?]

- @post_event: Continue

@meeting_dissent
title: Hear dissent before fixing the agenda
subtitle: Every tendency receives a hearing, even if no sharp public line emerges
on-arrival: {!
var repeated = Q.coalition_procedure === "Component council";
Q.coalition_procedure = "Component council";
Q.coalition_procedure_stage += 1;
Q.toilers_relation += repeated ? 1 : 4;
Q.religious_network_relation += repeated ? 1 : 4;
Q.iran_party_dissent -= repeated ? 1 : 2;
Q.independent_nationalists_dissent -= repeated ? 1 : 2;
if (repeated) Q.organizational_reach -= 1;
!}

The chair gives each component time to state what it will and will not support.
No common slogan emerges, but the secretariat learns where a future demand
would split the room and where compromise is still possible.

Allies who feared being overruled are more willing to remain at the table.
The {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} ends the
meeting with better knowledge rather than a sharper line.
[? if coalition_procedure_stage > 1: Hearings still keep allies in the room,
but organizers waiting for a decision increasingly see consultation itself as
a reason for delay. ?]

- @post_event: Continue

@meeting_oil
title: Put oil coordination on the agenda
subtitle: Oil offers common ground without resolving what each ally means by constitutional reform
on-arrival: {!
var repeated = Q.coalition_procedure === "Oil coordination";
Q.coalition_procedure = "Oil coordination";
Q.coalition_procedure_stage += 1;
Q.independent_nationalists_relation += repeated ? 1 : 4;
Q.religious_network_relation += repeated ? 1 : 2;
Q.oil_coalition_support += repeated ? 1 : 3;
if (repeated) Q.iran_party_dissent += 1;
!}

The delegates compare the language their organizations are willing to use on
oil. {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Independent nationalists{!</span>!} and {!<span class="term term-religious">!}religious-network{!</span>!} organizers find a shared
public emphasis even though their reasons for supporting it differ.

The coalition is better prepared to speak together on oil. Its machinery and
its other disagreements are unchanged.
[? if coalition_procedure_stage > 1: Oil coordination now produces fewer new
commitments. Constitutional organizers begin asking when the coalition will
return to the other undertakings on which it was founded. ?]

- @post_event: Continue
